but our view was the benefits that this  is likely to lead to in terms of  reducing uh impacts in education  outweighed those negative errors  so overall our assessment is combining  the marginal but  assessed benefit that jcvi made at  individual level  taking on board additionally the issues  around education  our view was the benefit exceeded the  risk to a sufficient degree that we are  recommending to our ministers in all  four nations  that they make a universal offer and i  want to stress the word offer  of vaccination to children  12 to 15 in addition to the ones that  have already been given it  so that is our current  advice to ministers and it is now with  them  to decide in each of the four nations uh  how they wish to respond just want to  add one additional thing and then i'd  like to ask dr rain and professor lim to  add from their sides and then turned to  my cmo colleagues  and that is that  we are really clear that it is very  important that um  this balanced decision of how the  benefits exceed the risks but the the  fact that these are closer than they are  in for example people in their 50s 60s  70s  are communicated  fairly and properly  to children young people and their  parents so that they can make a  considered decision that's very much  something we think is important and that  children young people and their parents  are supported in the decisions they make  and there is no stigmatization of people  either for accepting or not accepting  vaccination we do think this is really  critical and our colleagues in general  practice and in pediatrics and child  health will work with colleagues in  public health to try and help make sure  that that  balance of understanding is presented in  a an appropriate way that actually makes  sense and is comprehensible by people at  different ages  about what's going on so that is uh our  overall advice but that important rider  at the end  so i'd like to turn if i may to dr rain  just add some comments from mhra's point  of view  well thank you cmo and good afternoon as  the independent regulator for the uk for  medicines and vaccines we have  rigorously reviewed all the data on  safety quality and effectiveness of  pfizer and moderna for use in young  people and children 12 to 15 years old  and this data was also reviewed by the  commission on human medicines its expert  advisory group on vaccine benefit risk  and the pediatric medicines expert  advisory group all leading experts in  their field  over 2000 children were studied 2000 age  12 to 15 in the trials immunogenicity  studies  showed similar neutralizing antibody  levels in adolescents as in young adults  and these results together with the  clinical trial data showed a very high  level of short-term efficacy consistent  with that seen in adults and in fact  there was evidence that the vaccine  provides protection even before dose two  these are extremely positive results  we've continued our job with ongoing  proactive monitoring of safety and i'd  like to say a few words about suspected  side effects you will have seen our adr  report which comes out every week with  the latest data but there has been a  particular interest in reports of heart  inflammation myocarditis and  pericarditis these both happen very  rarely in the  general population and our job is to  continually review the balance of what  we observe against what we expect to see  we've undertaken a very thorough review  both of the uk and the international  reports there's a consistent pattern  there are slightly more  often frequently do we see cases in  young males and after the second dose  but overall the conclusion of our expert  advisors is that these are mild cases  individuals usually recover within a  short period of time with standard  treatment and our advice remains that  the benefits outweigh the risks of  getting vaccinated and this includes  those age 12 to 15.  thank you cmo thank you dr um professor  lim  thank you and thank you for this chance  to explain where we're at  you will remember that it was not that  long ago that we provided advice from  jcbi regarding  12 to 15 year olds and in particular  regarding 12 to 15 years who don't have  underlying health conditions that put  them at risk  we concluded that there was overall  marginal benefit from vaccination  we felt that that marginal benefit was  not sufficient to advise universal offer  or vaccination  i also want to stress today that jcbi  obviously recognizes the large impact  that the pandemic has had on our  children  and this is not simply because of direct  infection or illness on children but  because of the many other factors  involved in the pandemic not least  social isolation  missing school and being off school has  had a particularly profound impact on  our children  this is an impact not just on  educational attainment but also on  mental health and all the other learning  that comes from socially interacting  with our peers and colleagues and in  teachers as well  so an important question that arises  is to ask to what degree does  vaccination reduce  time off school for our children  the answer to that question is  influenced by many things  and it's particularly influenced by the  policies around school isolation  measures  school closures and school infection  control measures just as examples  those things are outside the remit of  jcvi  but they are important  and it was because of that that when we  gave our advice to ministers we  highlighted to ministers that there are  other factors that are worth considering  and suggested to ministers that they  could if they wish to  ask the cmos to assess the impact of  vaccination on these other factors  therefore today  the fact that the cmos have done this  assessment and this review is good news  for everybody because then  the wider impacts of vaccination have  been examined and i want to stress that  this by no means means that there is any  conflict between the advice provided by  jcvi  and the advice and decision that's been  provided by the cmos to the secretary of  state
 

